*********************
******Beginner*******
*********************

Q1. What is an array?

A collection of elements of the same type stored in contiguous memory locations.

Q2. What is the first index?

0.

Q3. What is random access?

Accessing an element directly using its index.

Q4. Complexity of accessing arr[i]?

O(1).

Q5. Can an array contain duplicate values?

Yes.

Q6. Can an array contain different data types?

A normal C++ array stores elements of the same type.

****************************
********Intermediate********
****************************

Q7. Why is insertion at the beginning O(n)?

Existing elements generally need to be shifted.

Q8. Why is accessing an array O(1)?

Because its contiguous memory layout allows direct address calculation.

Q9. Array vs linked list?

Array provides fast indexed access, while linked lists are better suited to certain insert/delete operations when the relevant node position is already known.

Q10. Array vs vector?

A built-in array has fixed size, while vector is dynamically resizable and provides useful STL operations.

*****************************
Tricky Interview Questions
*****************************

Q11. Why shouldn't you always initialize maximum as 0?

Because all elements could be negative.
Q12. What happens if you access outside the array bounds?

It's undefined behavior in C++.
